virglrenderer: scanout buffers as IOSurfaces, for QEMU's direct present

What: a single-level 2D buffer the guest makes for scanout (VIRGL_RES_BIND_SCANOUT)
in an 8-bit (BGRX, BGRA) or 10-bit (B10G10R10X2, B10G10R10A2) format, and not one of
QEMU's own 2D resources (y_0_top: the boot console's), lives in an IOSurface ('BGRA'
or 'l10r'), bound as a GL_TEXTURE_RECTANGLE texture: framebuffers
write and read it as before, and QEMU puts the IOSurface itself on the window
(omacvm-cocoa-present-direct.patch). Apple's GL binds an IOSurface only as a
RECTANGLE texture, so samplers read a 2D shadow, copied when a draw samples the
buffer after it changed (its view swizzle as for a texture its views share); the
shader blitter reads the shadow too (and gets the shadow's swizzle back), and the
cursor readback reads a RECTANGLE texture.
The guest's page flip completes in the guest without waiting for the Mac, so the
guest may draw into a buffer that QEMU still queues, that is on the window, or that
WindowServer still holds (it keeps the frame shown before the current one). Every
write into such a buffer is checked (draws and clears through its framebuffer, a
clear of it, blits, copies and uploads into it): the first write after QEMU took the
buffer for the screen (borrow_texture_for_scanout) moves a buffer still in use to a
free IOSurface with its picture copied on the GPU, as the guest redraws only what
changed; framebuffers attach it again. Spares are charged to the guest's GPU memory
budget as display memory, the shadow too (past the budget a spare is made all the
same: at most three a buffer, as nothing may write a buffer on screen); with three
spares all in use the oldest is let go (WindowServer frees it when done) for a new
one. Nothing waits.
QEMU waits for a fence made after the guest's last write into the buffer (at the end
of each submit or upload and before a plain switch of GL contexts, in the writing
context) before the buffer goes on the window: the guest's contexts and QEMU's are
different GL contexts. A shadow copied in one context is waited for in another.
New entry points: virgl_renderer_resource_get_iosurface,
virgl_renderer_resource_iosurface_fence, virgl_renderer_iosurface_fence_wait.
qemu.log every 5 s while moves, waits or shadow copies happen.
Why: QEMU's present drew every frame into an IOSurface of its own: 0.60 ms of GPU a
frame at 5120x2880 (72 ms/s at 120 Hz), more for 10-bit (half-float surfaces).
Upstream: not sent (macOS only).
Tests: VM A/B and the frame check (OMACVM_TEST_PACE_CHECK) in
docs/experiments/display-path.md.
